{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1509457891,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyNjk5NjU2MTI=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1945","origin":"github"},"title":"make plugins the 1st class citizen of datalad","message":"#### What is the problem?\n\nThis is not to be fixed right away but I just wanted to express in writing my whining a disturbance which plugins brought into the view and possibly just my not clear view on their purpose and difference in \"role\" from regular commands.\nAs far as I see them ATM, they are just the same \"datalad commands\" but defined differently and with the possibility for flexibly being added by the user/admin to become available.  The only argument I have heard so far on why they aren't full \"blown\" datalad commands, was \"they are just a python function\".  That is ok.  Our interfaces definitions are pretty much a \"function\" as well (well - glorified into a staticmethod of a class).  But those are just implementation details -- either they are full blown kids of the Interface with _args or a function with parameters described (hopefully) in a docstring. It should be e.g. possible to construct a \"command\" interface from a plugin definition (function) if needed.\n\nReciprocally I do not see why any interface command couldn't be considered a plugin and thus possibly participate in (not used by me yet) `--run-after` and `--run-before` dance.\n\nATM plugins are also an ugly duckling with `--show-plugin-help` and `--show-plugin-info` (bloody thing just did it and committed whenever I expected it just to show some kind of info):\n\n```shell\n(git)hopa:~pymvpa/pymvpa2-feedstock[bf]git\n$\u003e datalad plugin add_readme --show-plugin-info \nadd_readme(ok): /home/yoh/proj/pymvpa/pymvpa2-feedstock/README.rst (file)\nadd(ok): /home/yoh/proj/pymvpa/pymvpa2-feedstock/README.rst (file)\nsave(ok): /home/yoh/proj/pymvpa/pymvpa2-feedstock (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 1)\n  add_readme (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n```\n\nWhat triggered my whining to condence:\nPlaying a bit with conda, I liked how their interface for conda command just gets extended right away if you install additional `conda-build` or `conda-smithy` packages.  I do not see why, in principle, our interface could be extended with plugins, may be groupped into another section of --help-np, and something like --help-commands  providing concise listing of all the commands (without current 2 lines per each) similar to `datalad plugins` output\n\n#### What steps will reproduce the problem?\n\ntry to use `datalad plugin`","files":null}]}